# Media and figures

## Media {#media}

`![text](path)` shows an image, a video or an audio file. The text describes it for those who cannot see it.

:example
```
![Two boxes, from .nml to .html](/media/diagram.svg)
```

The path is relative to the page, or to the workspace's root with a leading `/`. A file of the workspace is copied to the output's `assets/`, and the page points to the copy; a URL is used as it is.

Options follow, in brackets, and a name and classes in braces:

:example
```
![The build](/media/diagram.svg)[width=50%]{#build}

[[build]] shows the build.
```

| Option | |
|:-------|:-|
| `width` | A CSS width, such as `50%` or `20rem`. |
| `type` | `image`, `video` or `audio`; by default, from the file's extension. |

A named medium is numbered with the page's other named media, apart from figures.

In Lua, [[elements#element|`nml.element`]] builds `media`, with the same `url`, `alt`, `type` and `width`; `ref` names it:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("media", {
  url = "/media/diagram.svg",
  width = "40%",
  alt = "The build",
  ref = "lua-build",
})`

[[lua-build]] is numbered.
```

## Figures {#figure}

`:figure`, a name and a caption, put the block after it in a numbered figure:

:example
```
:figure #pipeline From source to page
![](/media/diagram.svg)

[[pipeline]] sums it up.
```

The block can be anything: a code block, a table, a layout of several images. Rendered blocks — `:graph`, `:tex`, `:typst` — take a name and a caption themselves: see [[math-diagrams#figures]].

In Lua, a `figure` has a `caption` and a `ref`; its children are its body:

:example
```
:lua`nml.element("figure", {
  ref = "lua-figure",
  caption = "Built in Lua",
  nml.element("media", {
    url = "/media/diagram.svg",
  }),
})`

[[lua-figure]] sums it up too.
```
